[38;5;231m⏺[39m Server returns it. That settles the signature question: [38;5;153mlogin()[39m keeps [38;5;153m(username, password)[39m.

  Now the second question — the other half of the request, and the reason I called this architectural.

  [1mThe fork:[0m "track who logged in" needs a destination. Right now the only destination in this codebase is [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m,
  which is not tracking — it's debugging, and it vanishes when the tab closes.

  - [1mKeep it at [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m, just include the userId[0m — zero new structure, stays bounded-sized, and genuinely useful if
    all you want is visible-in-devtools confirmation while developing. Buys nothing durable; no one can answer "who
    logged in yesterday."
  - [1mA small [38;5;153msrc/tracking.js[39m module[0m — one [38;5;153mtrackEvent(name, payload)[39m seam that today writes to console and later points at
    whatever real backend you pick. New module, but a deliberately thin one. This is the piece the repo doesn't have,
    and it's why the task escalated. Cheap to change later: the call sites stay stable while the implementation behind
    them moves.
  - [1mPOST events to a real analytics/backend endpoint[0m — actually answers "who logged in." Needs an endpoint, error
    handling for when it fails, and a decision about whether a failed track blocks login. Most work, and it drags in
    privacy questions (a user ID in an analytics store is personal data).

  [1mMy recommendation: the [38;5;153msrc/tracking.js[39m seam.[0m It's the smallest thing that makes "track" mean something beyond a
  console line, and it puts the decision about a real backend off until you actually have one — without you having to
  touch every call site again when that day comes. If this app is a scratch fixture and you just want the ID visible,
  say so and option one is the right answer.
